1. Setup and Deployment Instructions:
-------------------------------------
=>Prerequisites:

AWS Account (eu-central-1)
Terraform CLI (v1.5+)
kubectl CLI (for EKS)
AWS CLI configured
Helm (for EKS ingress controllers)
Docker (for building container images)
Git repository for CI/CD

=> Terraform Infrastructure Deployment
--------------------------------------

Clone the Terraform repo:
git clone https://github.com/your-org/multi-tenant-terraform.git
cd multi-tenant-terraform

Initialize Terraform:

Terraform init
Plan infrastructure changes:
terraform plan -var-file=envs/prod.tfvars

Apply the infrastructure:
terraform apply -var-file=envs/prod.tfvars

Resources provision:
VPC with public & private subnets
EKS cluster (or ECS cluster)
Application Load Balancer (ALB)
RDS instance (PostgreSQL)
S3 bucket for artifacts
IAM roles and security groups
Namespaces & RBAC in EKS

=> Deploy Kubernetes Applications (EKS):
 -----------------------------------------

Configure kubeconfig:
aws eks --region eu-central-1 update-kubeconfig --name multi-tenant-cluster

Create team namespaces:
kubectl apply -f k8s/namespaces.yaml

Apply RBAC & Network Policies:

kubectl apply -f k8s/rbac.yaml
kubectl apply -f k8s/network-policies.yaml


Deploy applications via CI/CD:
-----------------------------

Configure GitHub Actions / GitLab CI / CodePipeline to build and push Docker images to ECR.
Deploy manifests to the respective namespaces.

=>ECS Deployment:
------------------
ECS service tasks are defined in Terraform ecs.tf.
ALB target groups are automatically associated with ECS services.
Deploy container tasks via Terraform or CodePipeline.

=>CI/CD Pipeline Deployment:
----------------------------

Terraform provisions:
Build runners (GitHub Actions / CodeBuild)
Artifact storage (S3)
Deployment automation to EKS/ECS
Pipelines trigger automatically on code push to the repository.

=>Design Decisions Justification:
| Component                              | Decision                                      | Rationale                                                               |
| -------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **EKS over ECS**                       | Chose EKS for Kubernetes-native multi-tenancy | Native namespace isolation, RBAC, and network policies for 20–50+ teams |
| **Terraform**                          | Infrastructure as code                        | Enables reproducible, version-controlled deployments                    |
| **ALB single entry point**             | Public ALB + private subnets                  | Central ingress, TLS termination, and reduced attack surface            |
| **RDS managed DB**                     | Managed PostgreSQL Multi-AZ                   | Simplifies backups, scaling, and high availability                      |
| **S3 for artifacts**                   | Centralized storage for container images/logs | Highly durable, scalable, and integrates with CI/CD                     |
| **Namespaces per team**                | Multi-tenancy isolation                       | Limits cross-team access and resource consumption                       |
| **Network Policies & Security Groups** | Fine-grained traffic control                  | Implements zero-trust architecture within VPC                           |

=>Security Best Practices Implementation:
------------------------------------------
IAM Roles for EKS Pods and ECS Tasks with least privilege.
RBAC in Kubernetes for namespace isolation per team.
Separate IAM policies for CI/CD pipelines (deploy vs read-only).

=>Network Security:
------------------
ALB in public subnets; workloads in private subnets.

Security Groups:
ALB: only port 443 open externally
Pods/Tasks: only allow required inbound/outbound
Kubernetes Network Policies enforce Pod-to-Pod communication boundaries.

=> Data Protection:
-------------------
TLS termination at ALB.
RDS encrypted at rest and S3 bucket encryption enabled.
Use AWS Secrets Manager / Parameter Store for sensitive data like DB passwords.

=>Observability & Compliance:
-------------------------------

CloudWatch / Prometheus for metrics.
Fluent Bit / FireLens for log collection to CloudWatch Logs.
OpenTelemetry for distributed tracing.
Audit logs for compliance: track all actions via CloudTrail.

=>Multi-Tenant Isolation:
--------------------------
Each team has a dedicated namespace (EKS) or ECS service cluster.
Resource quotas prevent one team from consuming all cluster resources.
Network policies prevent unauthorized cross-team traffic.
